{
  "case_id": "2006-06-480",
  "document_hash": "4f464a808a3fa4b8c53674660786fd34c63f3ab4a12d36254c60fb3c42c63f66",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2006-06-480",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4f464a808a3fa4b8c53674660786fd34c63f3ab4a12d36254c60fb3c42c63f66",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f464a808a3f-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:03 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Theodore B. Olson",
      "case_id": "2006-06-480",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4f464a808a3fa4b8c53674660786fd34c63f3ab4a12d36254c60fb3c42c63f66",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f464a808a3f-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The per se illegality rule for resale price maintenance is widely recognized to be outdated, misguided and anticompetitive. It should be replaced with the same rule of reason standard that applies to other forms of vertically imposed marketing restrictions."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2006-06-480",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4f464a808a3fa4b8c53674660786fd34c63f3ab4a12d36254c60fb3c42c63f66",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f464a808a3f-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: There was an argument made, Mr. Olson, that it is somewhat difficult to distinguish vertical from horizontal in this context, that in fact, the agreement that the manufacturer made with the dealers was more successful in getting a horizontal accord among the dealers than if the dealers had attempted it themselves, in which case some might have held back."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2006-06-480",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4f464a808a3fa4b8c53674660786fd34c63f3ab4a12d36254c60fb3c42c63f66",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f464a808a3f-0000-0005",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "He points out the drug industry after you got rid of -- after you got rid of resale price maintenance, the margins fell 40 percent. The drug stores it went down 20 percent. He says with blue jeans, alone, it saved American consumers $200 million to get rid of it."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2006-06-480",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4f464a808a3fa4b8c53674660786fd34c63f3ab4a12d36254c60fb3c42c63f66",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f464a808a3f-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: Wouldn't your argument also apply to a conspiracy among the New York dealers in this product just to fix prices? Because there's plenty of interbrand competition, I think. I don't think you can say it's absolutely clear that that would always be anti-competitive because they would also agree to provide additional services."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2006-06-480",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4f464a808a3fa4b8c53674660786fd34c63f3ab4a12d36254c60fb3c42c63f66",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f464a808a3f-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: I'm talking about a case in which it's the dealers who want to agree to provide extra services at higher prices as their method of better serving the public and they all agree that they have to be conscious about the competition from other brands. Why can we be absolutely certain that's always going to be harmful to the consumer?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. HUNGAR",
      "case_id": "2006-06-480",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4f464a808a3fa4b8c53674660786fd34c63f3ab4a12d36254c60fb3c42c63f66",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f464a808a3f-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. HUNGAR: Your Honor, the reason why we know that is always or almost always harmful is that the incentive at a horizontal level of a retailer cartel, just like the incentives of the participants in a manufacturing cartel --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ping amicus brief",
      "case_id": "2006-06-480",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4f464a808a3fa4b8c53674660786fd34c63f3ab4a12d36254c60fb3c42c63f66",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f464a808a3f-0001-0003",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. HUNGAR: As the Ping amicus brief, the Ping Golf Club Manufacturer amicus brief, indicates it's extremely expensive and inefficient to follow the Colgate regime, that for those manufacturers for whom resale price maintenance would be in effect a strategy like Leegin it's more efficient to do it in many circumstances by agreement, rather than the disruption that is entailed when you terminate a dealer without further discussion for discounting one item in order to keep your policy in place."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2006-06-480",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4f464a808a3fa4b8c53674660786fd34c63f3ab4a12d36254c60fb3c42c63f66",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f464a808a3f-0001-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 28
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Is that right? I mean, You really think that antitrust policy means when -- any arrangement that produce a higher price is bad?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Coykendall",
      "case_id": "2006-06-480",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4f464a808a3fa4b8c53674660786fd34c63f3ab4a12d36254c60fb3c42c63f66",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f464a808a3f-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, Your Honor, we would suggest that the horizontal conspiracy between Leegin as a retailer and the other retailers offering his products is more than just a rule of reason approach. That would be per se illegal under this Court's precedents."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2006-06-480",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4f464a808a3fa4b8c53674660786fd34c63f3ab4a12d36254c60fb3c42c63f66",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f464a808a3f-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Mr. Coykendall, the -- on the question -- you alleged in the complaint that there was some discounting allowed by, how do you pronounce it, Leegin?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Coykendall",
      "case_id": "2006-06-480",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4f464a808a3fa4b8c53674660786fd34c63f3ab4a12d36254c60fb3c42c63f66",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f464a808a3f-0002-0004",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And if resale price maintenance does act as it is theorized, to increase retailer services, some consumers will be worse off, they'll be paying for services they don't want."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Underwood",
      "case_id": "2006-06-480",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4f464a808a3fa4b8c53674660786fd34c63f3ab4a12d36254c60fb3c42c63f66",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f464a808a3f-0002-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The per se rule against resale price maintenance is different in at least three ways from other antitrust rules that this Court has overturned. First, unlike the other rules, it alone has been settled law for a century, reaffirmed over and over again by this Court."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2006-06-480",
      "confidence": 0.0,
